ype, so the boot and the time step are the caller's (packages/escher-driver/src/session.rs:14-161). `serve(state_dir: &Path, session: Session, idle_expiry: Duration) -> Result<(), SessionError>` hosts one session for the life of a process: it prepares the state directory and its socket as §Occupied Resources records (a socket file no process answers on is replaced, one a process answers on is `AlreadyRunning`, a path `std` refuses as too long is `StateDirTooLong`), answers one connection at a time on the calling thread with a 2 s read and write bound each — its accept loop non-blocking, polled every 25 ms — runs a `call` request through `Session::run` and nothing else, answering with the outcome or the refusal as one line of JSON, and on `stop`, or once no request has been answered for `idle_expiry`, drops the session and removes the socket file and the directory; every answered request — a refused or malformed one included — counts toward `served` and starts the idle count again, and a connection that breaks before its request is whole does neither; `IDLE_EXPIRY`, what a command line's host serves under, is 1,800 s (packages/escher-driver/src/host.rs:9-14; packages/escher-driver/src/host.rs:35-208). The lifecycle another process drives, and the call it sends: `attach(state_dir) -> Result<Hello, SessionError>` with `Hello { pid: u32, label: String, served: u64, idle_expiry_s: u64 }` — no socket file reads `NoSession`, a socket nothing answers on `Dead`, an answer outside the protocol `Protocol`; `stop(state_dir)` sends the stop request and waits, at most 5 s, until the state directory is gone (`Timeout` past it); `start(state_dir, host: std::process::Command, ready: Duration) -> Result<Started, SessionError>` with `Started { child: std::process::Child, hello: Hello }` attaches first (answered reads `AlreadyRunning`, nothing spawned), spawns `host` as configured and polls every 25 ms until the session answers, the host exits (`HostExited(code)`) or `ready` passes (`T