er-too-large` repeats `not-settled`'s (packages/escher-driver/src/error.rs:12-203; packages/escher-driver/src/error.rs:56-64; packages/escher-driver/src/error.rs:74-158). The wire is crate-private and versioned `v2` since 2026-10-10-driver-cli: one request line and one reply line per connection, `\n`-terminated — requests `hello v2`, `stop v2` and `call v2 <verb> [<name>=<kind>:<value>]…` (kinds `t` text · `n` whole number · `f` flag; the verb, the names and a text value percent-escaped byte for byte — every byte but an ASCII letter, a digit, `-`, `_`, `.` and `~` — and decoded strictly: uppercase hex only, no escape of a byte that needs none, the decoded bytes UTF-8, a number in its one spelling), at most 16,384 bytes where `v1` admitted 64, the widest call the schema admits being 15,360 bytes of values; replies `ok v2 pid={pid} label={label} served={n} idle_expiry_s={s}`, `ok v2 stopping`, `refused version` (a known word with another version) and `refused malformed` (anything else, a call the decoder does not read included), at most 128 bytes — the widest `hello` measures 122 — and, to a call, `ok v2 accepted <json>`, `ok v2 refused <json>` or `ok v2 oversize`, an answer bounded at 1,048,576 bytes on its JSON line alone and `frame` the one function that turns an answer into its reply; `{n}` counts every request the host answered before this one, refusals included; a refusal or a broken connection changes nothing, an `oversize` reply is no refusal — the call ran and what it did stands — and a `stop` takes effect once its reply is written (packages/escher-driver/src/wire.rs:12-299). On a non-unix target `serve`, `start`, `attach`, `stop` and — for a call the schema admits — `call` return `Unsupported`; a `Session` held in process works on every target. The command and refusal schema is stated once, as `'static` data; one `Session` method takes a `Call` — `run`, which executes it (below) — and since 2026-10-10-driver-cli a call reaches it from outside the process too